# Actor-bound API-key view capture evidence

Issue 63 identifies the API-key capture as historical bytes without observed
actor provenance. The owning producer is
`tests/card/mod-loader/oracle-capture-api-key-views.rb`. Its `as_bot` seed block
does not establish the actor of later renders: pinned `Auth::Proxy#as` restores
the proxy while initializing the current actor only if it was nil. Do not
retroactively label the existing capture as a bot capture.

Future acceptance requires the actual current and effective actor at every
render, their before/after stability, the observed bot identity and permission
verdicts, exact producer and authentication-source hashes, runtime versions,
and the database/target identity used for those observations. A validator must
compare these with explicit external expectations and reject missing,
malformed or mismatched evidence. Metadata completeness alone does not prove
the runtime, whole database snapshot, fixture provenance or full parity.

The producer must preserve existing capture bytes, refuse an existing output
destination, retain raw pre-seed authentication initialization state, and use
an explicit actor scope for new renders. It may clean up only probe cards it
created itself; it must not trash pre-existing probes. Rendering or cleanup
errors remain failures rather than successful expected-output strings.

The old capture remains available for explicitly named historical byte-shape
diagnostics. Those diagnostics cannot count as actor-qualified parity. Source
and synthetic validator tests do not authorize a live reference run or clear
issue 63. Fresh reference/provider execution requires its existing qualified
runtime, isolation and artifact-binding conditions.
